    "title": "LESSON 10: THE ACCUSATIVE",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Lesson 5 named the first role: the",
          "NOMINATIVE, worn by the subject and printed",
          "on every card.",
          "",
          "Here is the second. The ACCUSATIVE marks",
          "the thing acted on — the one seen, sought",
          "or defended.",
          "",
          "   subject        object",
          "   via            viam",
          "   inimīcus       inimīcum",
          "   oppidum        oppidum  (no change)",
          "",
          "   Mīles novam viam invenit.",
          "   = The soldier finds a new road.",
          "",
          "   Hic vir inimīcum meum dēfendit.",
          "   = This man defends my enemy."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Because the ending carries the job, the",
          "object is free to stand anywhere:",
          "   Glōriam mīles noster semper quaerit.",
          "   = Our soldier always seeks glory.",
          "",
          "Glōriam opens the line and is still the",
          "thing sought. Read the ending, not the place.",
          "",
          "Neuter words are the exception: in 'Parvum",
          "oppidum legiō nostra dēfendit', only the",
          "sense marks oppidum as the town defended.",
          "",
          "An adjective takes its noun's role with it:",
          "novam viam is one phrase, both accusative.",
          "",
          "Nouns of other families mark the object",
          "with -em: hostem, urbem. Their pattern",
          "arrives later."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "011",
    "trigger_word": "referō",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 11: THE PRESENT TENSE",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "A Latin verb carries its subject in the",
          "ending. No separate word for 'I' or 'he' is",
          "needed:",
          "",
          "   vocō     = I call",
          "   vocās    = you call",
          "   vocat    = he, she or it calls",
          "   vocāmus  = we call",
          "   vocātis  = you all call",
          "   vocant   = they call",
          "",
          "Six endings — -ō, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt.",
          "They hold across the whole verb system, in",
          "every tense that follows.",
          "",
          "   Cōnsul senātum in forum vocat.",
          "   = The consul calls the senate to the",
          "     forum."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "What changes is the vowel before those",
          "endings. Two families fill this tier.",
          "",
          "The -āre family keeps an a:",
          "   imperat, imperant  = commands, command",
          "The -ēre family keeps an e:",
          "   docet, docent      = teaches, teach",
          "",
          "   Pater fīlium virtūtem docet.",
          "   = The father teaches his son courage.",
          "",
          "Every verb card prints its family as a cue,",
          "-āre or -ēre, so a new verb tells you which",
          "vowel to expect before the ending.",
          "",
          "One present tense covers both 'he calls'",
          "and 'he is calling'. Latin does not split",
          "the two."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "012",
    "trigger_word": "fīlius",
    "trigger_card_number": 51,
    "title": "LESSON 12: THE IMPERATIVE",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Latin has a shape for giving an order, and",
          "it is the shortest form a verb owns: the",
          "stem with its family vowel, nothing after",
          "it.",
          "",
          "   -āre verbs:  vocā!  = call!",
          "   -ēre verbs:  tenē!  = hold!",
          "",
          "   Tenē viam, mīles, et castra pete!",
          "   = Keep to the road, soldier, and make",
          "     for the camp!",
          "",
          "Speaking to more than one person adds -te:",
          "",
          "   vocāte!   tenēte!",
          "",
          "Recognition only: knowing an order when one",
          "lands, never issuing one."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Two shapes sit close enough to confuse:",
          "   vocat  = he calls    (a statement)",
          "   vocā   = call!       (an order)",
          "",
          "The order carries no personal ending, and",
          "that missing -t is the whole difference.",
          "The plural pair runs the same way:",
          "",
          "   vocātis = you all call",
          "   vocāte  = call, all of you!",
          "",
          "Verbs outside these two families end the",
          "singular order in -e or -ī:",
          "   Pugnā et oppidum dēfende!",
          "   = Fight and defend the town!",
          "",
          "Four cut it shorter still: dīc (say),",
          "dūc (lead), fac (do), fer (bring)."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
